   P1(P1) 'Original parameter indexed over A and B',
   P2(A,C);

P2(A,C) = map(B_C, P1(A,*));
```
The `map` function creates a new parameter `P2` based on the original parameter `P1`. The second argument in `map`, `P1(A,*);`, indicates that the function should iterate over all combinations of `A` and `B` in `P1`.

Now, parameter `P2` contains the values from `P1` but indexed over sets `A` and `C`. The one-to-one mapping between `B` and `C` is used to create this new parameter.

Here's the complete GAMS code:

```gams
Set
   P1(A,B),
   A /A1, A2/
   B /B1, B2, B3/
   C /C1, C2, C3/;

Set
   B_C(B,C) 'One-to-one mapping between B and C';

B_C(B) = / #1.C1, #2.C2, #3.C3 /;

Parameter
