 Here is an example of blocking read being interrupted by a signal, with EINTR handling in C:

```c
#include <errno.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <signal.h>

#define BUF_SIZE 1024

void sigint_handler(int signo) {
    if (signo == SIGINT) {
        printf("Interrupted by signal SIGINT\n");
    }
}

int main() {
    char buf[BUF_SIZE];
    int fd = open("test.txt", O_RDONLY);
    if (fd < 0) {
        perror("open");
        exit(1);
    }

    struct sigaction sa;
    sa.sa_handler = sigint_handler;
    sa.sa_flags = 0;
    sigemptyset(&sa.sa_mask);
    if (sigaction(SIGINT, &sa, NULL) < 0) {
        perror("sigaction");
        exit(1);
    }

    while (1) {
        ssize_t nread = read(fd, buf, BUF_SIZE);
        if (nread < 0) {
            if (errno == EINTR) {
                printf("Read interrupted by signal, retrying...\n");
                continue;
            } else {
                perror("read");
                exit(1);
            }
        } else if (nread == 0) {
            break;
        }

        // process the data in buf
        // ...
    }

    close(fd);
    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the program opens a file "test.txt" and reads data from it in a blocking manner using the `read` function. If a signal (in this case, SIGINT) is received while `read` is blocking, it will return with errno set to EINTR. The program then catches this error and retries the read operation.

The signal handler `sigint_handler` simply prints a message indicating that the program was interrupted by a signal, but does not block the signal. This allows the read operation to be interrupted again and handled correctly.

 Here is an example of C++ code that uses the BSD socket API to read network packets from a socket on port 888:
```
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/socket.h>
#include <netinet/in.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <iostream>

const int PORT = 888;

int main() {
    int sockfd;
    struct sockaddr_in serv_addr;
    char buffer[256];

    // Create socket
    sockfd = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_DGRAM, 0);
    if (sockfd < 0) {
        std::cerr << "ERROR opening socket" << std::endl;
        return -1;
    }

    // Initialize socket address structure
    memset((char *) &serv_addr, 0, sizeof(serv_addr));
    serv_addr.sin_family = AF_INET;
    serv_addr.sin_addr.s_addr = INADDR_ANY;
    serv_addr.sin_port = htons(PORT);

    // Bind socket to address and port
    if (bind(sockfd, (struct sockaddr *) &serv_addr, sizeof(serv_addr)) < 0) {
        std::cerr << "ERROR on binding" << std::endl;
        return -1;
    }

    // Start listening for packets
    std::cout << "Listening on port " << PORT << "..." << std::endl;

    while (1) {
        socklen_t clilen = sizeof(struct sockaddr_in);
        struct sockaddr_in cli_addr;
        int n = recvfrom(sockfd, buffer, 256, 0, (struct sockaddr *) &cli_addr, &clilen);
        if (n < 0) {
            std::cerr << "ERROR on recvfrom" << std::endl;
            return -1;
        }
        buffer[n] = '\0';
        std::cout << "Received packet from " << inet_ntoa(cli_addr.sin_addr) << ":" << ntohs(cli_addr.sin_port) << ": " << buffer << std::endl;
    }

    close(sockfd);
    return 0;
}
```
This code creates a UDP socket and binds it to port 888. It then enters an infinite loop, where it uses the recvfrom() function to wait for packets to be received on the socket. When a packet is received, the function prints out the address and port of the sender and the contents of the packet.

Note that this is a basic example and does not include any error handling or cleanup code that would be necessary in a production environment. Additionally, this code is not platform-independent, it is written for Unix-like systems, specifically for Linux or MacOS.

Also, you need to include some error handling and cleaning code, and also make sure that the program has sufficient privileges to bind to the port 888 (usually you need to run the program as root).

You can use the recv() function instead of recvfrom() if you don't need to know the address of the sender.

You can also use TCP by replacing SOCK\_DGRAM with SOCK\_STREAM in the socket() function and replacing recvfrom() with recv() function.

 To create a 500x500px square with the top right corner rounded in After Effects, you can use the following expression for the `pathProperty` of a shape layer:

```javascript
// Create a square path
var squarePath = [
  [0, 0],
  [500, 0],
  [500, 500],
  [0, 500],
  [0, 0]
];

// Create a rounded rectangle path with the same dimensions
var roundedRectPath = [];
var radius = 50; // Set the corner radius here (in pixels)
for (var i = 0; i < squarePath.length; i++) {
  var point = squarePath[i];
  if (i === 0 || i === 2) {
    roundedRectPath.push(point);
  } else {
    var prevPoint = squarePath[i - 1];
    roundedRectPath.push(prevPoint);
    roundedRectPath.push([
      prevPoint[0] + (point[0] - prevPoint[0]) / 2,
      prevPoint[1] + radius
    ]);
    roundedRectPath.push(point);
  }
}

// Return the rounded rectangle path
roundedRectPath;
```

This expression creates a `squarePath` with the desired dimensions (500x500px), then creates a `roundedRectPath` with the same dimensions but with rounded corners. The top right corner radius is set to 50px, but you can change this value by modifying the `radius` variable. Finally, the expression returns the `roundedRectPath` so that it's used as the shape layer's path.

Note: Make sure you add this expression to the `Path` property of the shape layer.

