 Sure, here is a JavaScript function that computes the n-th Fibonacci number modulo p using matrix exponentiation:

```javascript
function fibMod(n, p) {
  // Define the Fibonacci matrix
  const F = [[1, 1], [1, 0]];

  // Matrix exponentiation
  function matrixExp(matrix, n) {
    if (n === 1) {
      return matrix;
    }

    const half = matrixExp(matrix, Math.floor(n / 2));
    const result = multiplyMatrices(half, half);

    if (n % 2 === 1) {
      return multiplyMatrices(result, matrix);
    } else {
      return result;
    }
  }

  // Matrix multiplication
  function multiplyMatrices(A, B) {
    const a = A[0];
    const b = B[0];
    const c = A[1];
    const d = B[1];

    return [
      [a * b + c * d, a * c + c * b],
      [b * a + d * c, b * c + d * d]
    ];
  }

  // Compute F^n mod p
  const fibMatrix = matrixExp(F, n);
  const [a, b] = fibMatrix[0];

  // Return the top-left element of F^n
  return a % p;
}
```

This function uses matrix exponentiation to efficiently compute the n-th power of the Fibonacci matrix, and then returns the top-left element of the resulting matrix, which represents the n-th Fibonacci number. The function also takes the modulus p at each step to ensure that the intermediate values do not exceed the range of safe integers.

Note that this function uses recursion to implement matrix exponentiation, so it may not work for very large values of n. However, it should work well for moderately large values of n and p.

