 Implementing the entire TCP protocol in TypeScript is beyond the scope of this platform, as it is a complex protocol with many different states and behaviors. However, I can certainly help you get started with parsing the IPv4 header and TCP header from the binary payload you have.

Assuming your `Uint8Array` contains the entire IPv4 packet, here's an example of how you can parse the IPv4 header and TCP header in TypeScript:
```typescript
interface IPv4Header {
  version: number;
  ihl: number;
  tos: number;
  totalLength: number;
  identification: number;
  flags: number;
  fragmentOffset: number;
  ttl: number;
  protocol: number;
  checksum: number;
  sourceIP: number;
  destinationIP: number;
}

interface TCPHeader {
  sourcePort: number;
  destinationPort: number;
  sequenceNumber: number;
  acknowledgmentNumber: number;
  headerLength: number;
  flags: number;
  windowSize: number;
  checksum: number;
  urgentPointer: number;
}

function parseIPv4Header(payload: Uint8Array): IPv4Header {
  const view = new DataView(payload.buffer);

  const version = view.getUint8(0) >> 4;
  const ihl = view.getUint8(0) & 0xF;
  const tos = view.getUint8(1);
  const totalLength = view.getUint16(2, true);
  const identification = view.getUint16(4, true);
  const flags = view.getUint16(6) >> 13;
  const fragmentOffset = (view.getUint16(6) & 0x1FFF) * 8;
  const ttl = view.getUint8(8);
  const protocol = view.getUint8(9);
  const checksum = view.getUint16(10, true);
  const sourceIP = view.getUint32(12, true);
  const destinationIP = view.getUint32(16, true);

  return {
    version,
    ihl,
    tos,
    totalLength,
    identification,
    flags,
    fragmentOffset,
    ttl,
    protocol,
    checksum,
    sourceIP,
    destinationIP,
  };
}

function parseTCPHeader(payload: Uint8Array, ipv4Header: IPv4Header): TCPHeader {
  const view = new DataView(payload.buffer);

  const sourcePort = view.getUint16(ipv4Header.ihl * 4, true);
  const destinationPort = view.getUint16((ipv4Header.ihl * 4) + 2, true);
  const sequenceNumber = view.getUint32((ipv4Header.ihl * 4) + 4, true);
  const acknowledgmentNumber = view.getUint32((ipv4Header.ihl * 4) + 8, true);
  const headerLength = view.getUint16((ipv4Header.ihl * 4) + 12, true) >> 4;
  const flags = view.getUint16((ipv4Header.ihl * 4) + 14, true);
  const windowSize = view.getUint16((ipv4Header.ihl * 4) + 16, true);
  const checksum = view.getUint16((ipv4Header.ihl * 4) + 18, true);
  const urgentPointer = view.getUint16((ipv4Header.ihl * 4) + 20, true);

  return {
    sourcePort,
    destinationPort,
    sequenceNumber,
    acknowledgmentNumber,
    headerLength,
    flags,
    windowSize,
    checksum,
    urgentPointer,
  };
}

// Example usage:
const ipv4Payload = new Uint8Array([
  0x45, 0x00, 0x00, 0x2c, 0x80, 0x00, 0x40, 0x06, 0xc0, 0xa8, 0x01, 0x02,
  0x0a, 0x01, 0x64, 0x0a,
]);

const ipv4Header = parseIPv4Header(ipv4Payload);
console.log(ipv4Header);

const tcpPayload = ipv4Payload.subarray(ipv4Header.ihl * 4);
const tcpHeader = parseTCPHeader(tcpPayload, ipv4Header);
console.log(tcpHeader);
```
In this example, we define interfaces for the IPv4 header and TCP header, and then implement functions to parse the headers from the binary payload. Note that this is just a minimal implementation that parses the most basic fields of the headers. There are many more fields and behaviors that you would need to implement to fully support TCP.

Also, keep in mind that implementing TCP is a complex task that requires careful attention to the details of the protocol. You should consult the relevant RFCs (RFC 793, RFC 1122, and RFC 3168) and consider using an existing library if possible.

