 Yes, in Node.js, you can implement a pull-based stream using the `stream` module's `Transform` class.

In a pull-based stream, the consumer (or "puller") controls the flow of data by requesting data from the stream when it is ready to process more. This is in contrast to a push-based stream, where the producer (or "pusher") sends data to the consumer as soon as it is available.

To create a pull-based stream using the `Transform` class, you can define a `_transform` method that is called whenever the consumer requests data. The `_transform` method should write data to the stream's `push` method, which will send the data to the consumer. Here's an example:
```javascript
const stream = require('stream');

class PullStream extends stream.Transform {
  constructor(options) {
    super(options);
    this._data = [];
  }

  _transform(chunk, encoding, callback) {
    // Add the chunk to the internal data array
    this._data.push(chunk);

    // If the consumer has requested data, push it to the consumer
    if (this._readableState.pulling) {
      this.push(this._data.shift());
    }

    // Call the callback to indicate that we are ready for more data
    callback();
  }

  // This method is called when the consumer requests data
  _read(size) {
    // Set the pulling flag to indicate that the consumer is ready for data
    this._readableState.pulling = true;

    // If there is data available, push it to the consumer
    if (this._data.length > 0) {
      this._readableState.pulling = false;
      this.push(this._data.shift());
    }
  }
}

// Example usage:
const pullStream = new PullStream();

pullStream.on('data', (chunk) => {
  console.log(`Received data: ${chunk.toString()}`);
});

pullStream.write('Hello, world!');
pullStream.write('This is a pull-based stream.');

// Request data from the stream
pullStream._read();
pullStream._read();

pullStream.end();
```
In this example, we define a `PullStream` class that extends the `Transform` class. The `_transform` method is called whenever data is written to the stream. We add the chunk to an internal data array and check if the consumer has requested data using the `_readableState.pulling` flag. If the consumer has requested data, we shift the first chunk from the data array and push it to the consumer using the `push` method.

The `_read` method is called when the consumer requests data using the `_read` method. We set the `_readableState.pulling` flag to indicate that the consumer is ready for data and check if there is data available in the internal data array. If there is data available, we shift the first chunk from the data array, set the `_readableState.pulling` flag to false, and push the data to the consumer using the `push` method.

Finally, we demonstrate how to use the `PullStream` class by writing data to the stream and requesting data from the stream using the `_read` method.

 Here is an example of a Metal compute kernel that performs a Gaussian blur on an image:

First, you will need to define a Metal compute pipeline state with a kernel function that performs the Gaussian blur:

```
#include <metal_stdlib>
using namespace metal;

kernel void gaussian_blur(texture2d<half, access::read> inTex [[texture(0)]],
                          texture2d<half, access::write> outTex [[texture(1)]],
                          constant GaussianBlurParams& params [[buffer(0)]],
                          uint2 gid [[thread_position_in_grid]])
{
    half4 color = half4(0);

    // Calculate the sum of the weights for the current pixel
    float sum = 0.0;
    for (int i = -params.radius; i <= params.radius; i++) {
        for (int j = -params.radius; j <= params.radius; j++) {
            float weight = gaussian_weight(i, j, params.sigma);
            sum += weight;
        }
    }

    // Calculate the weighted sum of the neighboring pixels
    for (int i = -params.radius; i <= params.radius; i++) {
        for (int j = -params.radius; j <= params.radius; j++) {
            float weight = gaussian_weight(i, j, params.sigma) / sum;
            color += inTex.sample(coord(gid.x + i, gid.y + j), params.texel_size) * weight;
        }
    }

    // Write the result to the output texture
    outTex.write(color, gid);
}

float gaussian_weight(int x, int y, float sigma) {
    float xx = x * x;
    float yy = y * y;
    float sigma_square = sigma * sigma;
    return (1.0 / (2.0 * M_PI * sigma_square)) * exp(-(xx + yy) / (2.0 * sigma_square));
}
```

Next, you will need to create a Metal compute pipeline state with this kernel function and set the necessary texture and buffer bindings.

Here is an example of how to do this in Swift:

```
