 Here's a Java program that prompts the user to enter two positive integers and displays their greatest common divisor (GCD) using Euclidean algorithm:

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class GCD {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.print("Enter first positive integer: ");
        int num1 = scanner.nextInt();

        System.out.print("Enter second positive integer: ");
        int num2 = scanner.nextInt();

        while (num1 < 0 || num2 < 0) {
            System.out.println("Both numbers must be positive. Please try again.");
            System.out.print("Enter first positive integer: ");
            num1 = scanner.nextInt();
            System.out.print("Enter second positive integer: ");
            num2 = scanner.nextInt();
        }

        int gcd = findGCD(num1, num2);
        System.out.println("The greatest common divisor of " + num1 + " and " + num2 + " is: " + gcd);
    }

    private static int findGCD(int num1, int num2) {
        if (num2 == 0) {
            return num1;
        }
        return findGCD(num2, num1 % num2);
    }
}
```

To run this program, copy the code into a file named `GCD.java`. Then open a terminal or command prompt, navigate to the directory containing the file, and compile it with `javac GCD.java`. After that, you can run the compiled program with `java GCD`.

This program uses recursion to implement the Euclidean algorithm for finding the GCD. It keeps calling the `findGCD` method with the second number and the remainder of dividing the first number by the second number until the second number becomes zero. At this point, the first number is the GCD.

