 Here's a sample program in Rust that reads URLs from a file, separates YouTube URLs from other URLs, and downloads the YouTube URLs using `yt-dlp`. This program uses the `reqwest` crate to make HTTP requests, the `select` crate to parse and filter URLs, and the `eyre` crate for error handling.

First, add the following dependencies to your `Cargo.toml` file:

```toml
[dependencies]
reqwest = { version = "0.11", features = ["blocking"] }
select = "0.4"
eyre = "0.6"
```

Then, create a new Rust source file (e.g. `main.rs`) and paste the following code:

```rust
use reqwest::blocking::get;
use select::document::Document;
use select::predicate::{Attr, Name};
use std::collections::HashSet;
use std::env;
use std::fs::File;
use std::io::{BufRead, BufReader};
use eyre::Result;

fn main() -> Result<()> {
    // Read URLs from a file
    let filename = "urls.txt";
    let file = File::open(filename)?;
    let reader = BufReader::new(file);
    let urls: HashSet<_> = reader.lines().filter_map(Result::ok).collect();

    // Download YouTube URLs using yt-dlp
    let yt_dlp_path = which::which("yt-dlp")?;
    for url in &urls {
        if is_youtube_url(url) {
            let output_path = format!("{}.mp4", url.rsplit_once('/').unwrap().1);
            let cmd = format!("{} --extract-audio --audio-format mp3 --output \"{}\" \"{}\"", yt_dlp_path, output_path, url);
            let status = std::process::Command::new("sh")
                .arg("-c")
                .arg(cmd)
                .status()
                .expect("failed to execute process");
            if !status.success() {
                eprintln!("Error downloading {}: command failed", url);
            }
        }
    }

    Ok(())
}

fn is_youtube_url(url: &str) -> bool {
    Document::from(get(url)?.text()?)
        .find(Attr("href", (Name("youtube.com"), Name("youtu.be"))))
        .next()
        .is_some()
}
```

This program reads URLs from a file named `urls.txt`, filters out YouTube URLs using the `is_youtube_url` function, and downloads the videos using `yt-dlp`. The downloaded videos are saved as MP3 files in the current directory.

Note that you need to have `yt-dlp` installed on your system and in your `PATH` for this program to work. You can install `yt-dlp` using the following command:

```
curl -L https://github.com/yt-dlp/yt-dlp/releases/latest/download/yt-dlp -o /usr/local/bin/yt-dlp && chmod a+rx /usr/local/bin/yt-dlp
```

Also note that this program uses the `which` crate to find the path of `yt-dlp` at runtime. If you prefer, you can hardcode the path to `yt-dlp` in the `yt_dlp_path` variable.

 Creating a Sierpinski triangle in XAML can be achieved by using a `Canvas` control and recursively defining the triangle shapes in the XAML markup. Here's a simple example of how you can create a Sierpinski triangle using XAML and code-behind:

1. Create a new WPF Application project in Visual Studio.
2. Replace the content of `MainWindow.xaml` with the following XAML markup:

```xaml
<Window x:Class="SierpinskiTriangle.MainWindow"
        xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        Title="Sierpinski Triangle" Height="600" Width="600" Background="White">
    <Canvas x:Name="canvas" Loaded="Canvas_Loaded" HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
</Window>
```

3. Replace the content of `MainWindow.xaml.cs` with the following C# code:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Windows;
using System.Windows.Media;
using System.Windows.Shapes;

namespace SierpinskiTriangle
{
    public partial class MainWindow : Window
    {
        private double _width;
        private double _height;

        public MainWindow()
        {
            InitializeComponent();
        }

        private void Canvas_Loaded(object sender, RoutedEventArgs e)
        {
            _width = canvas.ActualWidth;
            _height = canvas.ActualHeight;
            DrawTriangle(new Point(_width / 2, 0), _width, _height, 3);
        }

        private void DrawTriangle(Point point, double width, double height, int depth)
        {
            if (depth == 0) return;

            var topPoint = new Point(point.X, point.Y);
            var leftPoint = new Point(point.X - width / 2, point.Y + height / 2);
            var rightPoint = new Point(point.X + width / 2, point.Y + height / 2);

            var triangle = new Polygon
            {
                Points = new PointCollection {topPoint, leftPoint, rightPoint},
                Fill = Brushes.Black
            };

            canvas.Children.Add(triangle);

            DrawTriangle(topPoint, width / 2, height / 2, depth - 1);
            DrawTriangle(leftPoint, width / 2, height / 2, depth - 1);
            DrawTriangle(rightPoint, width / 2, height / 2, depth - 1);
        }
    }
}
```

This example creates a Sierpinski triangle by recursively dividing the triangle into smaller triangles until the desired depth is reached. The `DrawTriangle` method takes a starting point, width, and height, and depth as parameters. It calculates the vertices of the triangle and creates a `Polygon` object to represent the triangle. The method is called recursively for each smaller triangle.

The `Canvas_Loaded` method is called when the `Canvas` control is loaded, and it initializes the drawing process by calling the `DrawTriangle` method with the initial parameters.

